{"causes": ["The death of Qin Shi Huang in 210 BCE precipitated a power vacuum and widespread discontent that fueled numerous rebellions against the Qin dynasty.", "The tactical brilliance and growing military strength of rebel leaders like Xiang Yu created an insurmountable challenge for the Qin imperial forces.", "The inherent instability and harsh rule of the Qin dynasty had already created fertile ground for widespread uprisings across China."], "effects": ["Dong Yi's surrender at the Battle of Julu significantly diminished the Qin dynasty's military capacity, accelerating its eventual downfall.", "His subsequent enfeoffment as the King of Di contributed to the temporary fragmentation of China into the Eighteen Kingdoms under Xiang Yu's hegemony.", "His death at the hands of Liu Bang's forces helped consolidate power for the nascent Han dynasty, eliminating a potential rival during the Chu-Han Contention."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "dong-yi", "sourceName": "Dong Yi", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "zhang-han", "targetName": "Zhang Han", "context": "Dong Yi served as a subordinate general under Zhang Han in the final years of the Qin dynasty, fighting rebel forces from 209-207 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "xiang-yu", "sourceName": "Xiang Yu", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "dong-yi", "targetName": "Dong Yi", "context": "Xiang Yu's decisive victory at the Battle of Julu in 207 BCE directly led to Dong Yi's surrender and the collapse of Qin resistance."}, {"sourceSlug": "dong-yi", "sourceName": "Dong Yi", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "battle-of-julu", "targetName": "Battle of Julu", "context": "Dong Yi was a leading general in the Qin army that suffered a catastrophic defeat to Xiang Yu at the Battle of Julu in 207 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "dong-yi", "sourceName": "Dong Yi", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "china", "targetName": "China", "context": "Dong Yi's military career and subsequent kingship were entirely contained within the geographical boundaries of ancient China during the late Qin and early Han periods."}, {"sourceSlug": "liu-bang", "sourceName": "Liu Bang", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "dong-yi", "targetName": "Dong Yi", "context": "Liu Bang's forces, specifically led by Han Xin, attacked and killed Dong Yi in 203 BCE as part of the consolidation of power for the Han dynasty."}], "places": [{"name": "Julu, China", "role": "Major battle"}, {"name": "Gaonu, China", "role": "Capital of Di"}, {"name": "Xianyang, China", "role": "Qin Imperial Capital"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Dong Yi", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "dong-yi"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1038382", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dong_Yi_(Qin_dynasty)"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Dong Yi's role in the Battle of Julu and subsequent surrender was a major factor in the collapse of the Qin dynasty's military resistance, directly contributing to the fragmentation of China into the Eighteen Kingdoms. His brief reign as King of Di highlights the turbulent transitional phase before the establishment of the Han dynasty.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-31T04:14:47Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#41167444572", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Dong Yi was a Chinese military general of the Qin dynasty. Between 209 and 208 BC, when uprisings against the Qin dynasty broke out, Dong Yi, along with Zhang Han and Sima Xin, led Qin forces into battle against the various rebel groups and defeated some of them. However, they lost to rebel forces led by Xiang Yu in 207 BC at the Battle of Julu and were forced to surrender. After the rebels overthrew the Qin dynasty in 206 BC, China was divided into the Eighteen Kingdoms and the three surrendered Qin generals were made kings – Zhang Han as the King of Yong, Sima Xin as the King of Sai, and Dong Yi as the King of Di.", "newValue": "Dong Yi was a pivotal military general who served the collapsing Qin dynasty, most notably alongside Zhang Han and Sima Xin, during the tumultuous period of widespread rebellion that ultimately led to the dynasty's overthrow. His career, though brief, encapsulates the rapid disintegration of imperial authority and the brutal struggle for succession in the aftermath of Qin Shi Huang's death, playing a direct role in the resistance against burgeoning rebel forces that would eventually shape the Chu-Han Contention.

From 209 to 208 BCE, as the Qin Empire fractured under the weight of peasant uprisings and ambitious warlords, Dong Yi commanded significant Qin forces. He participated in campaigns that initially suppressed several rebel groups, demonstrating tactical prowess in a desperate effort to preserve the dynasty. However, the tide decisively turned in 207 BCE at the Battle of Julu, where Dong Yi, under the command of Zhang Han, faced the overwhelming forces of Xiang Yu. After a devastating defeat and being effectively cut off from relief, Dong Yi, along with Zhang Han and Sima Xin, was compelled to surrender to Xiang Yu, marking a critical turning point that severely weakened Qin's remaining military strength.

Following the Qin dynasty's collapse in 206 BCE, Xiang Yu, the dominant rebel leader, partitioned China into the Eighteen Kingdoms. Dong Yi was rewarded for his surrender and military experience by being enfeoffed as the King of Di (翟王), with his capital at Gaonu. This brief kingship, however, proved precarious. His fate was sealed shortly thereafter when Liu Bang, another prominent rebel leader and later founder of the Han dynasty, began to consolidate power. Dong Yi, along with Zhang Han and Sima Xin, was ultimately attacked and killed by Liu Bang's forces, specifically by Han Xin, in 203 BCE, as Liu Bang systematically eliminated rival kings to establish his new imperial order."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-31T04:14:47Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#41167444572", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The death of Qin Shi Huang in 210 BCE precipitated a power vacuum and widespread discontent that fueled numerous rebellions against the Qin dynasty.", "The tactical brilliance and growing military strength of rebel leaders like Xiang Yu created an insurmountable challenge for the Qin imperial forces.", "The inherent instability and harsh rule of the Qin dynasty had already created fertile ground for widespread uprisings across China."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-31T04:14:47Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#41167444572", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "203 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "203 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Dong Yi's surrender at the Battle of Julu significantly diminished the Qin dynasty's military capacity, accelerating its eventual downfall.", "His subsequent enfeoffment as the King of Di contributed to the temporary fragmentation of China into the Eighteen Kingdoms under Xiang Yu's hegemony.", "His death at the hands of Liu Bang's forces helped consolidate power for the nascent Han dynasty, eliminating a potential rival during the Chu-Han Contention."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-31T04:14:47Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#41167444572", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sourceSlug": "dong-yi", "sourceName": "Dong Yi", "verb": "COMMANDS", "targetSlug": "field-280", "targetName": "Military Leaders & Commanders", "context": "Dong Yi: Military command in military leaders & commanders"}, {"sourceSlug": "dong-yi", "sourceName": "Dong Yi", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "classical", "targetName": "Classical Era", "context": "Dong Yi was active during the Classical era"}], "newValue": [{"sourceSlug": "dong-yi", "sourceName": "Dong Yi", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "zhang-han", "targetName": "Zhang Han", "context": "Dong Yi served as a subordinate general under Zhang Han in the final years of the Qin dynasty, fighting rebel forces from 209-207 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "xiang-yu", "sourceName": "Xiang Yu", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "dong-yi", "targetName": "Dong Yi", "context": "Xiang Yu's decisive victory at the Battle of Julu in 207 BCE directly led to Dong Yi's surrender and the collapse of Qin resistance."}, {"sourceSlug": "dong-yi", "sourceName": "Dong Yi", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "battle-of-julu", "targetName": "Battle of Julu", "context": "Dong Yi was a leading general in the Qin army that suffered a catastrophic defeat to Xiang Yu at the Battle of Julu in 207 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "dong-yi", "sourceName": "Dong Yi", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "china", "targetName": "China", "context": "Dong Yi's military career and subsequent kingship were entirely contained within the geographical boundaries of ancient China during the late Qin and early Han periods."}, {"sourceSlug": "liu-bang", "sourceName": "Liu Bang", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "dong-yi", "targetName": "Dong Yi", "context": "Liu Bang's forces, specifically led by Han Xin, attacked and killed Dong Yi in 203 BCE as part of the consolidation of power for the Han dynasty."}]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-31T04:14:47Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#41167444572", "field": "detailsJson.places", "oldValue": [], "newValue": [{"name": "Julu, China", "role": "Major battle"}, {"name": "Gaonu, China", "role": "Capital of Di"}, {"name": "Xianyang, China", "role": "Qin Imperial Capital"}]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-31T04:14:47Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#41167444572", "field": "subjects", "oldValue": ["Military Leaders & Commanders"], "newValue": ["China", "Military History", "Qin Dynasty", "Chu-Han Contention", "Warfare", "Ancient History", "Rebellions", "Dynastic Collapse", "Kingship"]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-31T04:14:47Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#41167444572", "field": "frameworks", "oldValue": ["CONFLICT_AND_RESOLUTION", "GEOPOLITICAL_LINKAGE", "TEMPORAL_LINKAGE"], "newValue": ["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS", "CAUSE_AND_EFFECT", "COMPARATIVE_CIVILIZATIONS"]}], "_unsyncedEdits": true}
